  "worked_solution": "To find the coordinates of intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ Upon finding $t,$ we then find the coordinates of the intersection points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4t)-\\frac{32}{t} & =0 \\\\ 2t-\\frac{32}{t} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2{t}^{2}-32}{t} & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-32 & =0 \\\\ 2({t}^{2}-16) & =0 \\\\ 2(t+4)(t-4) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have the values of $t,$ we proceed to find $x$ and $y.$ \n\n- We start with $t=-4.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4t \\\\  & =4(-4) \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{32}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the first intersection point are $(-16,-8).$\n\n- We now move over to $t=4.$ For $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4t \\\\  & =4(4) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{32}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{4} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coordinates of the second intersection point are $(16,8).$\n\nTherefore, the curve $x=4t,\\,y=\\frac{32}{t}$ intersects the line $\\frac{1}{2}x-y=0$ at the points $(-16,-8)$ and $(16,8).$",
